## Proof

Let $p_k = \mathbb{P}(X = k)$ and $q_k = \mathbb{P}(Y = k)$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, \dots$. The probability generating function of $X$ is

$$G_X(t) = \mathbb{E}(t^X) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} p_k\, t^k.$$

Since $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} p_k = 1 < \infty$, for every $t \in [0,1)$ we have $\sum p_k t^k \le \sum p_k = 1$, so the series converges (absolutely) on $[0,1)$. Hence $G_X$ is a power series in $t$ with radius of convergence $R_X \ge 1$. Likewise $G_Y(t) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} q_k\, t^k$ is a power series with radius of convergence $R_Y \ge 1$.

**Claim:** $G_X(t) = G_Y(t)$ for all $t \in (0,1)$ implies $p_k = q_k$ for all $k$.

Define $H(t) = G_X(t) - G_Y(t) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (p_k - q_k)\, t^k$. This is a power series with radius of convergence $R \ge \min(R_X, R_Y) \ge 1$, and by hypothesis $H(t) = 0$ for every $t \in (0,1)$.

The interval $(0,1)$ contains infinitely many points with an accumulation point inside the disk of convergence (for instance, $t = 1/2$ is an accumulation point of $(0,1)$, and $|1/2| < 1 \le R$). By the **identity theorem for power series**: *if a power series $\sum a_k t^k$ with radius of convergence $R > 0$ vanishes on a set of points having an accumulation point strictly inside the disk $|t| < R$, then all its coefficients are zero.*

Applying this to $H$, we conclude $p_k - q_k = 0$ for every $k \ge 0$, i.e.,

$$\mathbb{P}(X = k) = \mathbb{P}(Y = k) \quad \text{for all } k = 0, 1, 2, \dots$$

Therefore $X$ and $Y$ have the same distribution. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
